+# Optional gateway whole-turn scheduling seam (proposal, not deployed)
+
+`gateway_turn_execution` is a proposed async native middleware kind. Registration
+uses the existing `PluginContext.register_middleware(kind, callback)` interface.
+No monkeypatch, new transport, auth principal, daemon, task queue or worker is added.
+
+The single call site is `session_finite.execute_finite_admission`, AFTER durable
+claim/preflight and BEFORE native execution. The authority remains the only owner
+of admission, per-session FIFO, unknown fencing, terminal settlement and delivery.
+
+```python
+async def schedule(*, next_call, profile_id, session_id, admission_id, input_bytes, physical_completion):
+    async with my_capacity_policy():
+        return await next_call()
+```
+
+Contract:
+
+- Runs under the owning profile's runtime scope. Native execution subsequently
+  restores its complete original provenance. No event/source/media/DB/runner
+  handles are handed to a plugin. Metadata is scheduling identity, not new authority.
+- The callback must await `next_call()` exactly once. Continuations close when their
+  callback exits and cannot be retained for later admission/replay.
+- Before execution, callback failure fails the claimed admission through the normal
+  authority failure path. This is deliberately NOT the fail-open lifecycle hook chain.
+- After execution starts, core joins the physical native coroutine. Native results
+  and errors dominate callback return values/errors (including lost acknowledgments).
+  Plugins cannot fabricate an alternative result or swallow a native exception.
+- Cancellation is propagated only after the started coroutine has quiesced. No
+  callback exception triggers fallback/re-execution. Long tools can therefore delay
+  cancellation/shutdown; freeing an occupied seat would be unsafe.
+- No callbacks preserves normal native execution. Native safe-worker policy still
+  suppresses plugin middleware. This does not authorize plugins in isolated workers.
+- A plugin must hold its own capacity reservation if native execution is unknown;
+  `ManagedExecutionUnknown` retains its native type and fencing path.
+- Policy admission waiting occurs AFTER claim, so an owner crash can leave an
+  unstarted scheduled turn unknown. No automatic replay is safe in that case.
+
+The implementation accesses native PluginManager callback storage like existing
+execution middleware. This proposed seam has not been accepted upstream and is
+not a promise of a stable API in currently released Hermes.
+
+Tests: `tests/gateway/test_turn_scheduling.py`. Additional add-on integration tests
+are in the separate hermes-for-teams-automatic worktree. No production owner was
+modified/restarted. Deployment requires independent review and owner-aware canary.
